# ALARM TIMER. A countdown for a text display: type "set 5:00" (or "set 5" for minutes) at a terminal, then "start",
# "pause" and "reset", or use a start/pause button and a reset button. The display shows mm:ss, always zero-padded
# ("01:09", never "1:9"), green while running, amber when paused. At zero it shows TIME! in red and sounds an alarm
# (out3) until reset. Tested in sandbox/alarm_timer.sbx.
#
# Patterns (CIRCUIT_PATTERNS.md):
#  * one writer per memory, and a feedback loop only one arithmetic step long: el -> add -> el. Its reset is a huge
#    negative number added for 0.1 s against ClampMin=0, so a reset and a tick can't fight over the memory;
#  * the start/pause state toggles through a XOR with its own memory (a pulse flips it);
#  * zero padding: concat("0", n), then a regex (.{2})$ keeps the last two characters;
#  * "mm:ss" is 5 characters, so it never scrolls on a text display.
name alarm_timer
box timer
note "ALARM TIMER: HOW IT WORKS" "Time left = set time - elapsed. Elapsed counts the 1 Hz pulses while running and time is left: el = el + pulse (an adder with ClampMin=0). For 0.1 s after a reset or a new time, -1000000 is added instead, which clamps it to 0.\nRunning: a memory flipped by a XOR whenever start/pause is pressed, 'start' is typed while paused, 'pause' or 'stop' while running, or reset while running.\nThe terminal: set M:SS or set M (minutes), start, pause, stop, reset. Anything else gets the list of commands.\nTime up: the alarm output is 1 while running at 0:00, and the display shows TIME! in red. (A one-time terminal message would need an edge detector, and those depend on the order parts update in, which you can't control in game.)"
note "WIRING" "in1 <- terminal signal_out, in2 <- start/pause button, in3 <- reset button\nout1 -> display set_text, out2 -> display set_text_color, out3 -> alarm (siren / light set_state), out4 -> terminal signal_in"
# --- terminal commands
c rSet regex Expression="^(?i)set ([0-9]+)(:[0-9]+)?$" UseCaptureGroup=true Output=1 FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
c rSec regex Expression="^(?i)set [0-9]+:([0-9]+)$" UseCaptureGroup=true Output=1 FalseOutput=0 ContinuousOutput=false
c rStart regex Expression="^(?i)start$" FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
c rStop regex Expression="^(?i)(pause|stop)$" FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
c rReset regex Expression="^(?i)reset$" FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
c rHelp regex Expression="^(?i)(set [0-9]+(:[0-9]+)?|start|pause|stop|reset)$" Output="" FalseOutput="Commands: set M:SS (or set M), start, pause, reset" ContinuousOutput=false
w in1 rSet.in rSec.in rStart.in rStop.in rReset.in rHelp.in
# set time = minutes x 60 + seconds
c k60 mem Value=60
c mins mul
c tsum add TimeFrame=0.1
c tot mem Value=300
w rSet.out mins.1
w k60.out mins.2 dmin.2 smod.set_modulus
w mins.out tsum.1
w rSec.out tsum.2
w tsum.out tot.in
# --- elapsed: el = el + pulse, one arithmetic step around the loop (memory first, then the adder: a loop through
# two arithmetic parts takes two frames and ends up as two counters taking turns). Reset: a huge negative input for
# 0.1 s against ClampMin=0.
c tick osc OutputType=Pulse Frequency=1
c gt0 greater
c a1 and
c dec and
c el mem Value=0
c inc add ClampMin=0
c zero mem Value=0
c rst or TimeFrame=0.1
c kBig mem Value=-1000000
c rneg mul
c dsum add
w tick.out a1.1
w run.out a1.2 xr.1 aStop.2 aRst.2
w a1.out dec.1
w gt0.out dec.2
w rReset.out rst.1
w in3 rst.2 aRst.1
w rSet.out rst.1
w rst.out rneg.1
w kBig.out rneg.2
w dec.out dsum.1
w rneg.out dsum.2
w el.out inc.1
w dsum.out inc.2
w inc.out el.in
# time left
c left sub ClampMin=0
w tot.out left.1
w el.out left.2
w left.out gt0.1 dmin.1 smod.in eq0.1
w zero.out gt0.2 eq0.2
# --- running: toggled by a XOR with its own memory
c run mem Value=0
c xr xor
c nrun not
c aStart and
c aStop and
c aRst and
c t1 or
c t2 or
c tg or
w run.out nrun.in
w rStart.out aStart.1
w nrun.out aStart.2
w rStop.out aStop.1
w aStart.out t1.1
w aStop.out t1.2
w in2 t2.1
w aRst.out t2.2
w t1.out tg.1
w t2.out tg.2
w tg.out xr.2
w xr.out run.in
# --- mm:ss, zero padded
c dmin div
c fmin floor
c smod mod Modulus=60
c z mem Value=0
c pm concat
c ps concat
c rm regex Expression="(.{2})$" UseCaptureGroup=true Output=1 FalseOutput=""
c rs regex Expression="(.{2})$" UseCaptureGroup=true Output=1 FalseOutput=""
c mmss concat Separator=:
w dmin.out fmin.in
w z.out pm.1 ps.1
w fmin.out pm.2
w smod.out ps.2
w pm.out rm.in
w ps.out rs.in
w rm.out mmss.1
w rs.out mmss.2
c sTime mem Value=TIME!
c dmux mux
w mmss.out dmux.signal_in0
w sTime.out dmux.signal_in1
w dmux.out out1
# --- time up: alarm while running at zero, the display says TIME! in red until reset
c eq0 equals
c alarm and
w eq0.out alarm.1
w run.out alarm.2
w alarm.out out3 cidx.1 dmux.set_input
c cidx add
w nrun.out cidx.2
c cmux mux
w cidx.out cmux.set_input
c green mem Value="120,255,140,255"
c amber mem Value="255,190,80,255"
c red mem Value="255,80,60,255"
w green.out cmux.signal_in0
w amber.out cmux.signal_in1
w red.out cmux.signal_in2
w cmux.out out2
# --- terminal replies (once per command)
c say1 regex Expression="^(?i)set " Output="Timer set." FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
c say2 regex Expression="^(?i)(start|pause|stop|reset)$" UseCaptureGroup=true Output=1 FalseOutput="" ContinuousOutput=false
w in1 say1.in say2.in
w say1.out out4
w say2.out out4
w rHelp.out out4
